Execution

Flags:
      --print-request           Write API shaped request bodies to stdout and exit.
      --resume                  Carry on after the last complete line in the --out file.
                                Streaming only. Under --id it skips the ids the file
                                answers, so a record whose content changed but whose id
                                did not keeps its old answer. It then rewrites the file
                                in input order, with the answered ids the input no
                                longer has kept after them, which --prune drops.
      --prune                   Drop the answered ids the input no longer has. Needs
                                --resume and --id.
      --unordered               Emit records as they complete. Streaming only.
      --stop-on VERDICTS        End the run at the first record with one of these
                                verdicts: error, fail or unsure, comma separated. An
                                error exits with its own code. Streaming only.
      --no-dedup                Ask every record even when its request repeats an
                                earlier one. Streaming only.
      --long-context            Raise the context limits to 262k tokens, slower and less
                                accurate. Also ONESIE_LONG_CONTEXT=1. Experimental on
                                TypeSafe's side.
      --mock FILE               Answer from this file instead of the API. Also
                                ONESIE_MOCK. The file is an object keyed by question id,
                                as {"answer":0.9,"team":"billing"}, or one line per
                                record in the shape -o json writes. A lone line that
                                carries id, line, model, usage, assert, abstain or an
                                error object is one record.
      --cache                   Store responses on disk and answer a repeated request
                                from them. Also ONESIE_CACHE=1.
  -j, --jobs N                  Records in flight at once. Default: 1.
      --timeout SECONDS         Seconds per attempt, 60 for a request with images unless
                                given. Default: 10.
      --retries N               Retries after a failed attempt. Default: 2.
      --max-retry-after SECONDS
                                Most seconds to wait out a server Retry-After. Default:
                                60.

How they work together:
  --jobs bounds records in flight. --timeout is seconds per attempt. Requests with
  images default to 60 seconds unless given. Retries happen after failed attempts,
  bounded by --retries. --max-retry-after caps seconds spent waiting for a server
  Retry-After. --long-context raises context limits, is slower and less accurate, and is
  experimental on TypeSafe's side. version reports the built in limits.

  --cache, or ONESIE_CACHE=1, stores successful responses on disk and reuses repeated
  requests. onesie cache reports entries and onesie cache clear empties them. Streams
  normally preserve input order. --unordered emits completed records immediately.
  --no-dedup asks every record even when its request repeats, independently of the disk
  cache. --stop-on=error ends at the first error with its exit code, or 6 for an
  unreadable record.

  --resume needs --out. Without --id, a stream continues after the last complete line.
  With --id it skips answered ids. A record whose content changes but whose id does not
  keeps its old answer. The file is rewritten in input order. Answered ids absent from
  input stay after them unless --prune drops them.

  --mock FILE, or ONESIE_MOCK=FILE, answers from fixtures without a key or network, so
  gate scripts can be tested for free. A mock file is one JSON object keyed by question
  id, which answers every record, as in {"urgent":0.9}, or one line per record in the
  shape -o json writes, so the --out file of a real run replays. Lines match records by
  id under --id and by position otherwise. Blank lines are refused, apart from those at
  the end. An entry {"error":503} makes the record an error, as that status does,
  {"error":"timeout"} as a timeout and {"error":"connection"} as a dropped connection.
  Each is retried as a live failure is, without the wait. --print-request writes API
  shaped request bodies and sends none. Question export writes configuration rather than
  answers. Mocks exercise execution and policy behavior. Request previews and question
  export do not execute those policies. Mocks are skipped beside --list-models, request
  input, --print-request and --print-questions.

